---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import "../../../styles/story.css";
import ReadingModes from "../../../components/ReadingModes.astro";
import Cite from "../../../components/Cite.astro";
import KpiStrip from "../../../components/story/KpiStrip.astro";
import ExplanationList from "../../../components/story/ExplanationList.astro";
import DecisionTimeline from "../../../components/story/DecisionTimeline.astro";
import QuoteCard from "../../../components/story/QuoteCard.astro";
import StorySources from "../../../components/StorySources.astro";
import { claim } from "../../../lib/claims.mjs";
import { event, n, chronology, words, inquiries, steps, explanations } from "../../../data/ai-chip-exports-2026-11-23-nvidia-reinstates-huang.mjs";
const story = "ai-chip-exports--2026-11-23-nvidia-reinstates-huang";
const short = (id) => claim(story, id).short.replace(/^(?:[A-Z][a-z]{2,3}\.?)[ 0-9,]*[0-9]{0,4}: /, "");
const cap = (s) => s.charAt(0).toUpperCase() + s.slice(1);
const timeline = chronology.map((e) => ({ date: e.date, title: cap(short(e.c)), current: e.current, cites: [{ c: e.c }] }));
---
<BaseLayout title={`${event.title} | The Catch`} current="Events">
  <div slot="locator" class="locator"><a href="/events/">Series</a><a href="/events/ai-chip-exports/">AI chip exports</a><span>November 23, 2026</span></div>
  <div class="content">
    <main class="story" id="story-root">
      <p class="story-kicker" data-layer="fact">AI chip exports &middot; November 23, 2026 &middot; updated {event.updated}</p>
      <h1 data-layer="fact" data-claims="board-vote-nov-22 trump-convinced">{event.title}</h1>
      <p class="story-dek" data-layer="fact">{event.dek}<Cite c="board-govt-input" /><Cite c="board-probe-continues" /></p>
      <ReadingModes />
      <nav class="story-toc" aria-label="In this story" data-layer="fact">
        <details class="toc-disclosure" open><summary><span>In this story</span><span class="toc-current" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
        <ol>
          <li><a href="#what-happened">What happened</a></li>
          <li><a href="#the-catch">The Catch</a></li>
          <li><a href="#eleven-days">From "does not intend" to a unanimous vote</a></li>
          <li><a href="#reaction">Who objects, and to what</a></li>
          <li><a href="#explanations">Every reading of "exonerated," tested</a></li>
          <li><a href="#next">What happens next</a></li>
          <li><a href="#records">The records</a></li>
        </ol>
        </details>
      </nav>

      <section id="what-happened">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happened</h2>
        <KpiStrip kpis={event.kpis} />
        <p data-layer="narrative">Nvidia's board voted unanimously on Sunday, November 22, to make Jensen Huang its president and chief executive again, {n.daysOut} days after it accepted his resignation, and announced the decision the next morning.<Cite c="board-vote-nov-22" /><Cite c="resigned-nov-12" /><Cite c="announced-nov-23" /> Huang, whom Nvidia's interim chief credited with building the company over 33 years, left after what the board's chair called personal behavior that broke the company's code of conduct.<Cite c="huang-33-years" /><Cite c="resign-reason" /> It included an affair with Alice Huang, whose company, <a href="/events/ai-chip-exports/2026-11-16-commerce-requires-licenses-for-chips-at-china-serving-data-centers/">Megaspeed</a>, bought large amounts of advanced Nvidia chips; people with knowledge of U.S. intelligence reports told The New York Times she kept in contact with an officer of China's Ministry of State Security during that time.<Cite c="alice-affair" /><Cite c="alice-mss-contact" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The board said its discussions and meetings with Huang left it satisfied with his candor and ability to lead, and that input from senior levels of the government, which it did not describe, made it confident his return poses no national security risk.<Cite c="board-candor" /><Cite c="board-govt-input" /> Its own investigation, opened on November 8 into reporting that Huang was the target of a foreign intelligence effort, will proceed to conclusion, the board said, and Nvidia says it is searching for a new chief compliance officer who will report to a board committee.<Cite c="board-opened-nov-8" /><Cite c="board-probe-continues" /><Cite c="cco" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Nvidia's stock closed up {n.stockUp} that Monday.<Cite c="stock-up-31" /> President Trump says he convinced the board, and that Huang was "exonerated."<Cite c="trump-convinced" /><Cite c="trump-exonerated" /> The Catch sets that word against what the government has said on the record.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="the-catch">
        <aside class="the-catch" data-layer="fact">
          <p class="tt-label">The Catch</p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <h3>"Exonerated" is Trump's word; the government's is "does not possess evidence"</h3>
              <p data-layer="narrative">Asked on CNBC what the attorney general had told him about Huang's potential criminal liability, Trump said "they did a pretty good investigation, and they felt that he himself was was exonerated," and then: "I don't know what word you want to use."<Cite c="trump-exonerated" /> Forbes headlined the interview "Trump Says Government Investigation Found Huang 'Did Nothing Wrong.'"<Cite c="forbes-headline" /></p>
              <p data-layer="narrative">The government's own words are narrower, and they reach the public mostly through Trump. His post that morning says the attorney general "has confirmed that the U.S. Government does not possess evidence that he has engaged in any unlawful conduct."<Cite c="trump-post-no-evidence" /> That describes the evidence the government holds; it is not a finding that cleared him. No Justice Department finding about Huang has been made public: a department official told reporters it has no evidence of wrongdoing by Huang or Nvidia, and the department declined to comment to Reuters.<Cite c="no-doj-statement" /></p>
              <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
                <figcaption><strong>What has been said about Huang's legal exposure, and by whom</strong></figcaption>
                <table class="data-table words-table">
                  <thead><tr><th>Who</th><th>Said</th></tr></thead>
                  <tbody>{words.map((w) => <tr><td>{w.who} <span class="on-record">{w.on}</span></td><td>{w.said}<Cite c={w.c} /></td></tr>)}</tbody>
                </table>
              </figure>
              <p data-layer="narrative">To be exonerated is to be cleared by an investigation. Asked on Fox News whether the Justice Department's investigation of Huang was closed, Attorney General Todd Blanche answered: "That assumes there's an investigation that's open. And that's never been discussed publicly."<Cite c="blanche-fox" /> U.S. officials told The Wall Street Journal that Blanche had told Trump Huang was not the target or subject of any Justice Department investigation, and a law-enforcement source told the Washington Examiner that the department never opened one on Huang or Nvidia in the Megaspeed case.<Cite c="wsj-not-target" /><Cite c="examiner-never-opened" /> Both accounts are anonymous, and Blanche did not confirm them; by those accounts, there was no case against Huang to clear him of.<Cite c="blanche-fox" /></p>
              <p data-layer="narrative">Nor did Trump say he had promised the board Huang would not be charged. "I didn't use the word prosecuted," he told CNBC; "we believe he's fine. He's absolutely fine. I gave them enough assurance," adding that "you can only give the assurance as to what you know."<Cite c="trump-assurance" /> The board had needed a statement that Huang was not facing criminal charges, according to people cited by the Times, and Trump told the Times he checked with Blanche and the director of national intelligence first: "I can't do this and then all of a sudden have somebody get indicted for, for whatever."<Cite c="board-needed-two" /><Cite c="trump-indicted-worry" /></p>
              <p data-layer="narrative">What remains open is a set of separate inquiries, each with its own subject.<Cite c="doj-megaspeed-case" /><Cite c="board-probe-continues" /><Cite c="sec-probe" /><Cite c="california-probe" /><Cite c="missouri-probe" /></p>
              <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
                <figcaption><strong>The inquiries still open after the reinstatement</strong></figcaption>
                <table class="data-table">
                  <thead><tr><th>Who</th><th>Into what</th></tr></thead>
                  <tbody>{inquiries.map((r) => <tr><td>{r.who}</td><td>{r.into}{r.c.map((id) => <Cite c={id} />)}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
                </table>
              </figure>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </aside>
      </section>

      <section id="eleven-days">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">From "does not intend" to a unanimous vote</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">On November 17, Huang wrote that he would be honored to return if the board decided it was in the company's interest; the same day, on Nvidia's earnings call, the board's chair, Harvey Jones, said the board did not intend to reinstate him.<Cite c="huang-offer" /><Cite c="jones-no-reinstate" /> By then Nvidia's stock had fallen {n.stockDown} from Huang's November 8 admission of an affair, and big investors were pressing: Altimeter Capital and Atreides Management, which hold about $2.1 billion of the stock, had asked the board to keep his return on the table, and a petition claimed more than 200,000 signatures, a shareholder's letter to the board says.<Cite c="stock-fell-33" /><Cite c="investor-campaign" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Trump says the fall made his job easier: "when the stock starts dropping 40, 50%, it was a lot easier for me to do it."<Cite c="trump-easier" /> He asked Vice President JD Vance to talk to Huang and work with the board, according to people cited by the Times, who say the board needed two things: an assurance that the administration did not see Huang as a national security threat, and a statement that he was not facing criminal charges.<Cite c="vance-asked" /><Cite c="board-needed-two" /> On Saturday, November 21, Blanche told Trump and Vance that Huang was not a target or subject of any Justice Department investigation, according to officials cited by the Journal, and on Sunday Trump posted that he hoped the board would rehire Huang; it voted that day.<Cite c="wsj-not-target" /><Cite c="trump-post-nov-22" /><Cite c="board-vote-nov-22" /></p>
        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>From the board's investigation to Huang's return</strong></figcaption>
          <DecisionTimeline entries={timeline} heading="What happened" />
        </figure>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The board had weighed a different national security question before. On November 8 it said it had no reason to believe national security or company secrets had been compromised; on November 23 it judged that his return poses no risk, on the strength of the government's input.<Cite c="board-nov8-confidence" /><Cite c="board-govt-input" /> Asked whether the board would have brought Huang back without him, Trump said:<Cite c="trump-convinced" /></p>
        <QuoteCard speaker="President Trump" when="November 23, 2026" where="Interview with CNBC">
          <p>"Yeah, the answer is no. I believe my intervention was very important."<Cite c="trump-convinced" /></p>
        </QuoteCard>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The White House says he was "totally involved."<Cite c="wh-involved" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="reaction">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Who objects, and to what</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Republicans objected as well as Democrats. Sen. John Curtis of Utah asked who in the government weighed in, what they told the board and on what basis it found no risk; Sen. John Cornyn of Texas asked, "No national security risk? Based on what?"; and the Republican chairs of the House China, Intelligence and Foreign Affairs committees said the reinstatement does not "erase the national security threats and questions that must be addressed."<Cite c="curtis-questions" /><Cite c="cornyn" /><Cite c="gop-chairs" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Rep. Hakeem Jeffries, the House Democratic leader, called the decision "indefensible, irresponsible and incomprehensible" and pledged to investigate "business malfeasance by NVIDIA and corrupt abuse of power by Howard Lutnick and the Department of Commerce"; his corruption charge is aimed at the commerce secretary, not Huang.<Cite c="jeffries" /> Sen. Chuck Schumer says Trump put "his own financial interests" first. Trump's accounts have traded Nvidia shares at least {n.trades} times since January 2025, by CNBC's count, and Nvidia went on to pledge {n.ballroom} to Trump's planned White House ballroom; the White House says independent advisers manage his portfolio.<Cite c="schumer" /><Cite c="trump-trades" /><Cite c="ballroom" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Trump calls Huang "a Great Patriot and National Treasure," and Blanche says "the right decision was reached."<Cite c="trump-patriot" /><Cite c="blanche-companies" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="explanations">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Every reading of "exonerated," tested</h2>
        <ExplanationList entries={explanations} />
      </section>

      <section id="next">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happens next</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Hours after the announcement, Commerce filed a rule adding Megaspeed and six other companies to the Entity List, so that selling them anything covered by its Export Administration Regulations needs a license it will presume to deny; the rule cites the risk that they divert advanced computing chips and gives no reason for its timing.<Cite c="entity-list" /> Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent had said <a href="/events/ai-chip-exports/2026-11-16-commerce-requires-licenses-for-chips-at-china-serving-data-centers/">Commerce "has the pen"</a> on blacklisting Megaspeed, and on November 19 Sen. Pete Ricketts introduced a bill to force a decision within 30 days.<Cite c="bessent-pen" /><Cite c="ricketts-bill" /></p>
        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>The dates ahead</strong></figcaption>
          <table class="data-table">
            <thead><tr><th>When</th><th>What</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>{steps.map((s) => <tr><td>{s.when}</td><td>{s.what}{s.c.map((id) => <Cite c={id} />)}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
          </table>
        </figure>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Huang's House interviews were set before his return, after the <a href="/events/ai-chip-exports/2026-11-19-house-committee-releases-china-chip-license-data/">committee released Commerce's China chip-license table</a>.<Cite c="house-interviews" /> Democrats preparing to investigate Nvidia have widened the inquiry to Trump's role in bringing Huang back, The Washington Post reports.<Cite c="dems-widen" /> The hearings can ask Curtis's question, who gave the board its assurance; they do not close the Justice Department's Megaspeed case or the SEC, California and Missouri inquiries.<Cite c="curtis-questions" /><Cite c="doj-megaspeed-case" /><Cite c="sec-probe" /><Cite c="california-probe" /><Cite c="missouri-probe" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="records">
      <StorySources event={event.slug} />
      </section>
    </main>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .story-figure .data-table td, .story-figure .data-table th { word-break: normal; overflow-wrap: normal; hyphens: none; }
  .words-table th:first-child, .words-table td:first-child { width: 34%; }
  .on-record { display: block; font-size: 0.8em; opacity: 0.75; margin-top: 0.2em; }
</style>
